st a humanitarian organization you  have in the International System a  unique role of of  governing the Geneva conventions which  are effectively are the rules of War  historic role you have there can I ask  you with that hat on the situation in  Ukraine only in the last few hours the  UN Commission of inquiries which is  looking into Ukraine has talked about  war crimes how worried are you that the  rules of war in Ukraine and elsewhere  are no longer being respected we are of  course worried in Ukraine and globally  as you rightly say we are involved with  the parties to the conflict with the  Armed Forces with the security forces to  have international humanitarian law  respected and there is no conflict in  the world where we don't see disrespect  the question is how we work afterwards  with the party how we try to influence  them and as you know it is really the  template of confidential dialogue the  methodology to really go to the  Commanders uh to the Ministers of  Defense with our findings on the ground  and to ask for better behavior for  change of behavior for accountability  within the armed forces and here I think  in Ukraine and elsewhere it's a huge uh  it's a huge challenge between the  necessity to protect civilian  populations and the military objectives  to defeat the adversary we always see  the balance tilt towards military logic  instead of protection logic of civilians  and this is what challenges us each and  every day but in Ukraine and elsewhere  we really try to forge our ways into the  mindset and into the thinking and into  the military orders in order to allow  International humanitarian law to at  least have some relevance in the  conflict and then I should say you  course our first worries are the people  themselves the families those who are  immediately affected by military  operations and I I think it's it is  important that more consideration  everywhere in the world is taken to  respect those basic rules  in this week you end up speaking with  presidents and prime ministers and  ministers are you worried that the war  in Ukraine is taking up all the  conversation when there are so many  other crises from previous years in  Yemen in Syria in Africa which haven't  been sold which are still there and no  one's talking about indeed there is a  real inequity of attention which is  probably the first big problem and  consequence of the war in Ukraine I have  difficulties except for the parties to  the yemeni the Syrian the sahelian  conflicts to find any interest  whatsoever also from political leaders  to discuss these issues because all the  attention is on Ukraine and then you  have the real-time uh effect of this  conflict the  impact on the world economy the fact  that many of the poorest countries who  have already experienced famine  difficulties to cope with multiple  challenges like climate change and  pandemic are now double and triple  affected by the real-time Ripple effects  of the Ukrainian crisis and so there is  a discrepancy on the one side everybody  looks and finances and tries to do work  in Ukraine and on the other hand there  are an increasing number of Forgotten  conflicts which is worrisome because you  never know when these conflicts will  come back to the international attention  because of Deep Impact that they still  have on societies it's a grim picture  Peter Mauer president of the  international Committee of the Red Cross  thank you for joining us here on Al  Jazeera you're just finishing 10 years  in the job and obviously you look at the  state of the world now compared with 10  years ago there are so many raging  conflicts that were not here a decade  ago Rob James thanks very much our  diplomatic editor James Bay is outside  the UN headquarters there
 

